course: dictionary
lesson: 8367
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one the writer means. Roundabout is a good example. It can be a place where cars go round, a spinning toy in a playground, or an adjective meaning not direct.
explain: «Think of a playground roundabout with three children holding on at different distances» uses roundabout as a toy. The children hold on and ride it, so it must be the spinning platform. Ask yourself: what is the thing doing in this sentence?
explain: «He took a roundabout route to avoid the busy road» is different. Roundabout describes the route, and a route that is not direct still makes sense here, so the adjective meaning is the right one.
fact: A roundabout is a circular junction where cars go around a middle island and leave at different roads.
fact: In the USA, a roundabout junction is often called a traffic circle or rotary.
fact: On a playground, a roundabout is a round platform that spins while children ride on it. In the USA it is called a merry-go-round.
fact: As an adjective, roundabout means not direct, as in «a roundabout way».
fact: A roundabout word can be a euphemism: «A euphemism is a soft or roundabout word we use instead of a plain word that feels too hard, rude or sad.»
exercise: «On a playground roundabout, a push at the edge turns it more easily than a push near the middle.» In this sentence, what is a roundabout? | *A round platform that spins for children to ride | A junction where cars go around a middle island | A way that is not direct | A cake sold at the summer fair
exercise: At the roundabout, Dad took the second exit. What is a roundabout here? | A fairground ride with painted horses | A spinning playground toy | *A junction where cars go around a middle island and leave at different roads | A long, winding path
exercise: «He took a roundabout route to avoid the busy road.» What does roundabout mean in this sentence? | Covered in paint | *Not direct | Round and smooth | Very fast
test: In «a roundabout way», what kind of word is roundabout? | A verb | An adverb | A noun | *An adjective
test: At a roundabout, who must give way? | Traffic already on it, who give way to drivers joining | Pedestrians, who always cross the middle | Nobody, because it is a race | *Drivers joining, who give way to traffic already on it
exam: In «carrying you round like a passenger on a smooth, silent roundabout», what is the Earth's spinning compared to? | Driving a car fast on a motorway | *Riding a smooth, silent spinning ride | Swimming in a quiet pool | Waiting for a bus at a stop
